\section{Methods}{

All sine and triangle methods are evaluated with closed-form expressions;
no hourly temperatures are generated.
\itemize{
\item \code{"average"}: \eqn{\max(0, (T_{max} + T_{min}) / 2 - T_b)} (McMaster &
Wilhelm 1997, method 1). With an upper threshold the cutoff is applied to
the daily mean.
\item \code{"single_sine"}: a sine wave between \code{tmin} and \code{tmax} over 24 h
(Baskerville & Emin 1969).
\item \code{"single_triangle"}: a symmetric triangle between \code{tmin} and \code{tmax}
(Lindsey & Newman 1956; Sevacherian et al. 1977).
\item \code{"double_sine"}, \code{"double_triangle"}: the day is split into two
half-day segments: \code{tmin} to \code{tmax} of the same day, then \code{tmax} to the
minimum of the following day (\code{tmin_next}). Each half contributes half a
day (Allen 1976; Zalom et al. 1983).
}
}

\section{Upper threshold cutoffs}{

With an upper threshold \eqn{T_u}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"horizontal"}: development continues at the constant rate reached at
\eqn{T_u}; the area above \eqn{T_u} is removed.
\item \code{"vertical"}: no development while temperature exceeds \eqn{T_u}; the
whole area under the curve during those hours is removed.
\item \code{"intermediate"}: development decreases linearly above \eqn{T_u} with
slope -1 (the area above \eqn{T_u} is subtracted a second time), with the
rate floored at zero. The floor only matters when temperatures exceed
\eqn{2T_u - T_b}.
}

The three cutoffs coincide when temperatures stay below \code{upper} and give
different results otherwise; they are not interchangeable.
}

\section{Missing values and boundaries}{

Missing \code{tmin} or \code{tmax} give \code{NA} for that day. Double methods need the
minimum of the next day; when \code{tmin_next} is \code{NULL} it is taken from the
following element of \code{tmin}, and the last day is \code{NA}. Supply \code{tmin_next}
explicitly to close the series (for a cyclic climatological year pass
\code{c(tmin[-1], tmin[1])}). If the following minimum is above the current
maximum, the second half-day segment is evaluated between the two values in
increasing order.
}

